-- Ahrefs Web Analytics: the Privacy Policy, Cookie Policy, Children's Privacy Notice and Your Privacy Choices
-- say it counts visits (no cookies, only with consent, never for Kids profiles or pages with codes), and What's new.
-- src/data/legalPages.json has the same. Each page changes only while it still has the built-in wording.

update public.legal_pages set sections = $lw$[{"heading": "Questions or concerns?", "paragraphs": ["Reading this Privacy Notice will help you understand your privacy rights and choices. We are responsible for making decisions about how your personal information is processed. If you do not agree with our policies and practices, please do not use our Services. If you still have any questions or concerns, please contact us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "Summary of key points", "paragraphs": ["This summary provides key points from our Privacy Notice; you can find out more details about any of these topics in the numbered sections below.", "What personal information do we process? When you visit, use, or navigate our Services, we may process personal information depending on how you interact with us and the Services, the choices you make, and the products and features you use. See section 1.", "Do we process any sensitive personal information? Some information may be considered \"special\" or \"sensitive\" in certain jurisdictions, for example your racial or ethnic origins, sexual orientation, religious beliefs, biometric or financial data. We don't collect any of those. The only information we hold that some laws treat as sensitive is your account login information.", "Do we collect any information from third parties? Only from Stripe, which tells us whether your payments succeed and how your membership stands. We don't buy information about you or get it from social networks or data brokers.", "How do we process your information? We process your information to provide, improve, and administer our Services, communicate with you, for security and fraud prevention, and to comply with law. We may also process your information for other purposes with your consent. We process your information only when we have a valid legal reason to do so. See section 2.", "In what situations and with which parties do we share personal information? Only with the service providers that help us run LiteraryWorm, and in the specific situations described in section 4. We don't sell your personal information or share it for advertising.", "What don't we do? We don't sell your information or share it for advertising, we don't show ads, and we don't use your reading, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models. Our only analytics is Ahrefs Web Analytics: it sets no cookies, runs only if you allow analytics, and never runs for Kids profiles.", "How do we keep your information safe? Connections use HTTPS, our database lets each account read only its own data, passwords and PINs are kept only as one-way hashes, and you can add two-step sign-in or passkeys.", "However, no electronic transmission over the internet or information storage technology can be guaranteed to be 100% secure, so we cannot promise or guarantee that hackers, cybercriminals, or other unauthorized third parties will not be able to defeat our security and improperly collect, access, steal, or modify your information. See section 9.", "What are your rights? Depending on where you are located geographically, the applicable privacy law may mean you have certain rights regarding your personal information. See section 11.", "How do you exercise your rights? The easiest way is in Account, where you can download a copy of your data, change it, or delete it, or by contacting us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. We will consider and act upon any request in accordance with applicable data protection laws."]}, {"heading": "1. What information do we collect?", "paragraphs": ["Personal information you disclose to us. In Short: We collect personal information that you provide to us.", "We collect personal information that you voluntarily provide to us when you register on the Services, when you participate in activities on the Services, or otherwise when you contact us.", "Personal Information Provided by You. The personal information that we collect depends on the context of your interactions with us and the Services, the choices you make, and the products and features you use. It includes your email address, display name, password, date of birth, contact preferences (marketing emails) and authentication data, and what you read, save and share in the app.", "Section 18 lists exactly what we keep.", "Sensitive Information. We do not collect biometric data, financial data, or other special categories of personal information.", "Payment Data. Payments are handled by Stripe (https://stripe.com/privacy), which collects and stores your card details; we never see or store your full card number. We keep the plan you chose, your billing day, Stripe's ids for your customer record and subscription, and whether each payment succeeded.", "If you buy a gift membership, we keep its code, plan, number of months and price, and whether and when it was redeemed (and, for a gift that starts on a later day, when we reminded the buyer).", "A gift can come with up to 10 LiteraryWorm books the buyer picks: we keep which ones with the code, Stripe receives their ids with the payment, the email we send the recipient names them, and redeeming the gift adds them to the redeemer's library.", "If you ask us to email a gift to someone, Stripe receives their email address and your message with the payment, and we keep both only until the email has gone; only the buyer can see the code, and we record which account redeemed it.", "Social Media Login Data. We don't offer registration or log in with social media accounts.", "All personal information that you provide to us must be true, complete, and accurate, and you must notify us of any changes to such personal information.", "Information automatically collected. In Short: Some information — such as your Internet Protocol (IP) address and browser and device characteristics — is collected automatically when you visit our Services.", "Log and Usage Data. Our hosting and backend providers (Vercel and Supabase) automatically record service-related, diagnostic and performance information when you access the Services, in log files. This may include your IP address, browser type, the date and time of requests, the pages and files requested, and error reports. It is used to keep the Services secure and working, not for advertising, and we don't build profiles from it.", "Location Data. We don't collect your device's location, and we don't use GPS or similar technologies.", "Google API. We use the Google Books API only for book information: our server sends it search words or an ISBN, book ids and the names of authors people follow, never who's asking. Our use of information received from Google APIs will adhere to Google API Services User Data Policy, including the Limited Use requirements."]}, {"heading": "2. How do we process your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We process your information to provide, improve, and administer our Services, communicate with you, for security and fraud prevention, and to comply with law. We may also process your information for other purposes only with your prior explicit consent.", "To facilitate account creation and authentication and otherwise manage user accounts. We may process your information so you can create and log in to your account, as well as keep your account and household profiles in working order.", "To deliver and facilitate delivery of services to the user. We may process your information to provide you with the requested service, such as keeping your place in a book across devices, your bookmarks, highlights, journal, saved words, lists, Up next queue, goals and stats.", "To respond to user inquiries/offer support to users. We may process your information to respond to your inquiries and solve any potential issues you might have with the requested service.", "To send administrative information to you. We may process your information to send you details about your account, changes to our terms and policies, and other similar information.", "To enable user-to-user communications. We may process your information if you choose to use any of our offerings that allow for communication with another user, such as reading logs, book clubs and shared lists. A book club's calendar link lets your calendar app read the club's meetings (their names, times and notes) from us; the link is yours, we don't store it, and it stops working if you leave the club.", "To send you marketing and promotional communications, only if you turned on marketing emails. You can turn them off at any time in Account. For more information, see section 11.", "To show you recommendations. Book recommendations and reading stats are worked out in your browser from your own reading; they aren't used for advertising.", "To protect our Services. We may process your information as part of our efforts to keep our Services safe and secure, including fraud monitoring and prevention and limiting how often our features can be used.", "To save or protect an individual's vital interest. We may process your information when necessary to save or protect an individual's vital interest, such as to prevent harm.", "We don't process your information to deliver targeted advertising."]}, {"heading": "3. What legal bases do we rely on to process your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We only process your personal information when we believe it is necessary and we have a valid legal reason (i.e., legal basis) to do so under applicable law, like with your consent, to comply with laws, to provide you with services to enter into or fulfill our contractual obligations, to protect your rights, or to fulfill our legitimate business interests.", "If you are located in the EU or UK, this section applies to you. The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and UK GDPR require us to explain the valid legal bases we rely on in order to process your personal information. As such, we may rely on the following legal bases to process your personal information:", "Consent. We may process your information if you have given us permission (i.e., consent) to use your personal information for a specific purpose, such as marketing emails or a Kids profile. You can withdraw your consent at any time (see section 11).", "Performance of a Contract. We may process your personal information when we believe it is necessary to fulfill our contractual obligations to you, including providing our Services or at your request prior to entering into a contract with you.", "Legitimate Interests. We may process your information when we believe it is reasonably necessary to achieve our legitimate business interests and those interests do not outweigh your interests and fundamental rights and freedoms: to diagnose problems and prevent fraudulent activities, and to keep the Services secure and working.", "Legal Obligations. We may process your information where we believe it is necessary for compliance with our legal obligations, such as to cooperate with a law enforcement body or regulatory agency, exercise or defend our legal rights, or disclose your information as evidence in litigation in which we are involved.", "Vital Interests. We may process your information where we believe it is necessary to protect your vital interests or the vital interests of a third party, such as situations involving potential threats to the safety of any person.", "If you are located in Canada, this section applies to you. We may process your information if you have given us specific permission (i.e., express consent) to use your personal information for a specific purpose, or in situations where your permission can be inferred (i.e., implied consent). You can withdraw your consent at any time.", "In some exceptional cases, we may be legally permitted under applicable law to process your information without your consent, including, for example:", "if collection is clearly in the interests of an individual and consent cannot be obtained in a timely way;", "for investigations and fraud detection and prevention;", "for business transactions provided certain conditions are met;", "if it is contained in a witness statement and the collection is necessary to assess, process, or settle an insurance claim;", "for identifying injured, ill, or deceased persons and communicating with next of kin;", "if we have reasonable grounds to believe an individual has been, is, or may be victim of financial abuse;", "if it is reasonable to expect collection and use with consent would compromise the availability or the accuracy of the information and the collection is reasonable for purposes related to investigating a breach of an agreement or a contravention of the laws of Canada or a province;", "if disclosure is required to comply with a subpoena, warrant, court order, or rules of the court relating to the production of records;", "if it was produced by an individual in the course of their employment, business, or profession and the collection is consistent with the purposes for which the information was produced;", "if the collection is solely for journalistic, artistic, or literary purposes;", "and if the information is publicly available and is specified by the regulations.", "We may disclose de-identified information for approved research or statistics projects, subject to ethics oversight and confidentiality commitments."]}, {"heading": "4. When and with whom do we share your personal information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We share information only with the service providers that help us run LiteraryWorm, and in the situations described in this section.", "Service providers. Supabase stores the data above and runs our server functions. Vercel hosts the website.", "Mailgun sends our emails and the book club invitations members send, so it receives the address each email goes to and the email's contents.", "The emails confirm your email address, reset your password, and tell you when a book you set Remind me on is out. There's a weekly list of new books from authors you follow, and a monthly round-up of what's new in LiteraryWorm if you turn it on.", "If you turn it on, a weekly report of how your Kids profiles read: their names, reading and listening time, days read, books finished and any weekly goal.", "A reminder the day before a book club you're in meets, and a note when one of its meetings moves or is cancelled: the club's name and book and the meeting's name, times and note, with the meeting as a calendar file.", "If you turn it on, a daily email of new posts in your book clubs: each club's name and book, the chapters you've reached that have new posts, how many and the display names of who wrote them; never what they wrote.", "A reminder three days before a gift membership you bought for a later day starts (its plan, months and start day; never the code), and a gift membership a member asked us to email to you (its code, plan and months, the buyer's display name and any message they wrote).", "And an email confirming that your account was deleted.", "We turn off Mailgun's open and click tracking.", "Our service providers may use your information only to provide their service to us, except where this section says they handle it under their own policy.", "If you turn on notifications on a device, the notification about a book you set Remind me on (its title and author, and the profile's name) goes through your browser's push service (Google for Chrome and Android, Apple for Safari, Mozilla for Firefox, Microsoft for Edge), encrypted so that only your browser can read it.", "You choose in Account → Preferences which kinds your devices get; besides release days they can be a club meeting in the next day (the club's and meeting's names), new posts in your clubs (the club's name, how many, the chapter and who wrote them; never what they said), an evening reading-streak nudge (a profile's name and run of days; for this we keep each device's time zone) and a grown-up's answer to a Kids book request (the profile's name and the book).", "Stripe processes payments. When you add a payment method, Stripe receives your name, email address, card details and billing address, and handles them under its own privacy notice (https://stripe.com/privacy).", "Termly provides our cookie consent banner and our privacy request forms. Your browser loads the banner from Termly on each visit, so Termly receives those requests, including your IP address, and your consent choice is kept in your browser. Termly handles this under its own privacy policy (https://termly.io/our-privacy-policy/). It never handles payment details: Stripe does.", "Ahrefs Web Analytics counts visits so we can see which pages people use. If you allow analytics in the consent banner, your browser sends Ahrefs (analytics.ahrefs.com) the pages you visit, their titles, the site you came from, your screen size and language, and links you follow to other sites. Ahrefs also receives your IP address and browser details with each request.", "We send page addresses without anything after ? or #, though a form you send, such as a search, can include it. Ahrefs sets no cookies and keeps nothing in your browser. It's off while a Kids profile is in use and on pages whose links carry codes, such as password resets, invitations and unsubscribe links.", "Ahrefs handles this under its own privacy policy (https://ahrefs.com/legal/privacy-policy). To stop it, choose Consent Preferences at the bottom of any page and decline analytics.", "Anthropic (Claude) writes book summaries and content notes, answers Ask the book questions, translates passages you select, writes Who's who and writes chapter quizzes and Previously… recaps (see section 6).", "Google Books answers searches and book details through our server, which sends only the search words or ISBN, book ids and the names of authors people follow, never who's asking. Book covers and the Google Books preview load from books.google.com directly in your browser, and Google sets third-party cookies there (see the Cookie Policy).", "LibriVox recordings stream from the Internet Archive directly from your browser. The app's fonts load from Google Fonts the same way, so Google receives your IP address with those requests.", "Other members. Reading logs are public, with your display name. Book club posts are seen by the club's members. If you invite someone to a club by email, the invitation shows them your display name and the club's name, book and finish date; we send it through Mailgun and keep only a one-way fingerprint of their address for 30 days (so the same address isn't invited again within a week), never the address itself.", "Lists you share can be read by anyone with the link. Staff with the right access can see account details to run the service and help you.", "Our support team. To help with a question, LiteraryWorm staff with the customer service role (and admins) can look up an account by its email address.", "They see the name and email, when the account was made, whether the email is confirmed and two-step sign-in is on, the plan and its billing state (never card details), how many profiles there are, this month's listening against the plan, and the devices keeping books offline. They never see your library, reading, notes, highlights or searches. Every lookup is recorded with who made it and when, and the record is deleted after a year.", "Business Transfers. We may share or transfer your information in connection with, or during negotiations of, any merger, sale of company assets, financing, or acquisition of all or a portion of our business to another company."]}, {"heading": "5. Do we use cookies and other tracking technologies?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We use only essential cookies and similar technologies, described in our Cookie Policy. Because we use the Google Books API, Google sets third-party cookies from books.google.com when book covers and previews load.", "When you first visit, a banner from Termly asks for your cookie choice, and you can change it at any time with Consent Preferences at the bottom of every page. Because we use only essential cookies, declining changes nothing about how the Services work.", "The technologies we use help us maintain the security of our Services and your account, keep you signed in, and let the app work offline.", "We don't permit third parties or service providers to use online tracking technologies on our Services for advertising, and we don't use Google Analytics. With your consent, we count visits with Ahrefs Web Analytics, which sets no cookies and never runs for Kids profiles (see section 4).", "Google Books covers and the Google Books preview load from books.google.com, and Google sets its own third-party cookies there, under Google's Privacy Policy. We don't receive them. To stop them, block third-party cookies in your browser; LiteraryWorm keeps working.", "Specific information about the technologies we use and how you can refuse certain cookies is set out in our Cookie Policy."]}, {"heading": "6. Do we offer artificial intelligence-based products?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We offer products, features, or tools powered by artificial intelligence, machine learning, or similar technologies.", "As part of our Services, we offer features powered by artificial intelligence (\"AI Products\"). The terms in this Privacy Notice govern your use of the AI Products within our Services.", "Use of AI Technologies. We provide the AI Products through one third-party service provider (\"AI Service Provider\"): Anthropic (Claude). We don't use OpenAI, Hugging Face, Google Cloud AI, DeepSeek or Gemini to process your information. Figma Make was used only to design the app's screens and never receives your information. You must not use the AI Products in any way that violates the terms or policies of the AI Service Provider.", "Our AI Products. Book summaries: short and detailed summaries of books in our catalog, written the first time anyone asks, from the book's details; no personal information is sent.", "Content notes: for each of our titles, how much violence, death, frightening scenes, language, sexual content, alcohol and drugs, prejudice and self-harm it has, written the first time anyone asks, from the book's details; no personal information is sent, and the notes are kept so everyone sees the same ones.", "Ask the book: answers to your questions about a public-domain book. We send your question and the book's text up to your chapter; we don't send your name, email or account details.", "Translate: a passage you select in a public-domain book, in another language. We send only the words you selected (they must be in that chapter), nothing about you, and keep nothing.", "Who's who: the characters you've met in a public-domain book so far. We send only the book's text up to your chapter, nothing about you, and keep the list so the next reader at that chapter gets the same one.", "Previously…: in the player, a recap of a public-domain book up to the chapter you've reached. We send only the text of the chapters before it, nothing about you, and keep the recap so the next listener there gets the same one.", "Chapter quizzes: a few questions about a chapter of a public-domain book, written for young readers. We send only that chapter's text, nothing about you, and keep the quiz so every reader of that chapter gets the same one; your score is saved with your profile.", "Ask the book, Translate and Who's who aren't offered to Kids profiles; summaries, content notes, recaps and quizzes are. Summaries, content notes, answers, translations, Who's who, recaps and quizzes are labelled as written by AI.", "How We Process Your Data Using AI. All personal information processed using our AI Products is handled in line with this Privacy Notice and our agreement with the AI Service Provider. We don't use your reading history, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models."]}, {"heading": "7. How do we handle your social logins?", "paragraphs": ["We don't offer registration or log in using social media accounts. If we add it, we'll update this Privacy Notice first."]}, {"heading": "8. How long do we keep your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We keep your information for as long as necessary to fulfill the purposes outlined in this Privacy Notice unless otherwise required by law.", "We will only keep your personal information for as long as it is necessary for the purposes set out in this Privacy Notice, unless a longer retention period is required or permitted by law (such as tax, accounting, or other legal requirements). No purpose in this notice will require us keeping your personal information for longer than the period of time in which users have an account with us. Section 16 lists how long we keep each kind of information.", "When we have no ongoing legitimate business need to process your personal information, we will either delete or anonymize such information, or, if this is not possible (for example, because your personal information has been stored in backup archives), then we will securely store your personal information and isolate it from any further processing until deletion is possible."]}, {"heading": "9. How do we keep your information safe?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We aim to protect your personal information through a system of organizational and technical security measures.", "We have implemented appropriate and reasonable technical and organizational security measures designed to protect the security of any personal information we process. For example, connections use HTTPS, and access rules in our database let each account read only its own data.", "Passwords and profile PINs are kept only as one-way hashes. You can turn on two-step sign-in or log in with a passkey, staff can see only what their role needs, and every support lookup is recorded.", "However, despite our safeguards and efforts to secure your information, no electronic transmission over the Internet or information storage technology can be guaranteed to be 100% secure, so we cannot promise or guarantee that hackers, cybercriminals, or other unauthorized third parties will not be able to defeat our security and improperly collect, access, steal, or modify your information.", "Although we will do our best to protect your personal information, transmission of personal information to and from our Services is at your own risk. You should only access the Services within a secure environment."]}, {"heading": "10. Do we collect information from minors?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We do not knowingly collect data from or market to children under 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction, except through Kids profiles that a parent or guardian creates on their own account.", "Account holders must be 18 or older. We do not knowingly collect, solicit data from, or market to children under 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction, nor do we knowingly sell such personal information.", "By using the Services, you represent that you are at least 18 or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction or that you are the parent or guardian of such a minor and consent to such minor dependent's use of the Services through a Kids profile. The Children's Privacy Notice explains what Kids profiles keep.", "If we learn that personal information from users less than 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction has been collected without that consent, we will deactivate the account and take reasonable measures to promptly delete such data from our records. If you become aware of any such data, please contact us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "11. What are your privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: Depending on your state of residence in the US or in some regions, such as the European Economic Area (EEA), United Kingdom (UK), Switzerland, and Canada, you have rights that allow you greater access to and control over your personal information. You may review, change, or terminate your account at any time, depending on your country, province, or state of residence.", "In some regions (like the EEA, UK, Switzerland, and Canada), you have certain rights under applicable data protection laws.", "These may include the right (i) to request access and obtain a copy of your personal information, (ii) to request rectification or erasure; (iii) to restrict the processing of your personal information; (iv) if applicable, to data portability; and (v) not to be subject to automated decision-making.", "If a decision that produces legal or similarly significant effects is made solely by automated means, we will inform you, explain the main factors, and offer a simple way to request human review. In certain circumstances, you may also have the right to object to the processing of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below.", "We will consider and act upon any request in accordance with applicable data protection laws.", "If you are located in the UK and are unhappy with how we have handled your personal information, you can make a complaint directly to us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. This is in addition to the rights you have under the UK General Data Protection Regulation and the Data Protection Act 2018.", "What happens after you complain: we will acknowledge your complaint within 30 days of receiving it, investigate without unjustifiable or excessive delay, keep you informed of progress and explain the outcome.", "If you are not happy with our final response, you can refer your complaint to the Information Commissioner's Office, the UK supervisory authority. Website: ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint. Helpline: 0303 123 1113. Post: Information Commissioner's Office, Wycliffe House, Water Lane, Wilmslow, Cheshire, SK9 5AF.", "If you are located in the EEA or UK and you believe we are unlawfully processing your personal information, you also have the right to complain to your Member State data protection authority or UK data protection authority.", "If you are located in Switzerland, you may contact the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner.", "Withdrawing your consent: If we are relying on your consent to process your personal information, which may be express and/or implied consent depending on the applicable law, you have the right to withdraw your consent at any time. You can withdraw your consent at any time in Account (for marketing emails and Kids profiles) or by contacting us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below.", "However, please note that this will not affect the lawfulness of the processing before its withdrawal nor, when applicable law allows, will it affect the processing of your personal information conducted in reliance on lawful processing grounds other than consent.", "Opting out of marketing and promotional communications: You can unsubscribe from our marketing and promotional communications at any time by turning off marketing emails in Account, by clicking on the unsubscribe link in the emails that we send, or by contacting us.", "We may still communicate with you, for example to send you service-related messages that are necessary for the administration and use of your account (each kind of email can be turned off in Account → Preferences, and the monthly What's new email, the weekly Kids reading report, book club meeting emails and club post emails also have a one-click unsubscribe link that works without signing in), to respond to service requests, or for other non-marketing purposes.", "Account Information. If you would at any time like to review or change the information in your account or terminate your account, you can log in and go to Account. There you can change your details, download a copy of your data, and delete profiles or your whole account. Deleting your account deletes its information from our databases straight away; copies in our providers' backups are removed when those backups expire.", "Cookies and similar technologies: we use only essential cookies, so declining cookies in Consent Preferences changes nothing about how the Services work. Blocking third-party cookies in your browser stops Google's cookies, and LiteraryWorm keeps working.", "Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out and removes books saved for offline. The Cookie Policy explains each one.", "If you have questions or comments about your privacy rights, you may email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "12. Controls for Do-Not-Track features", "paragraphs": ["Most web browsers and some mobile operating systems and mobile applications include a Do-Not-Track (\"DNT\") feature or setting you can activate to signal your privacy preference not to have data about your online browsing activities monitored and collected. At this stage, no uniform technology standard for recognizing and implementing DNT signals has been finalized.", "As such, we do not currently respond to DNT browser signals. We do honour Global Privacy Control, described below. We don't track you across other websites in any case. If a standard for online tracking is adopted that we must follow in the future, we will inform you about that practice in a revised version of this Privacy Notice.", "California law requires us to let you know how we respond to web browser DNT signals. Because there currently is not an industry or legal standard for recognizing or honoring DNT signals, we do not respond to them at this time.", "Global Privacy Control: We recognize and honor Global Privacy Control (GPC) signals. If you use a browser or extension that supports GPC, we will treat this as a valid request to opt out of the sale or sharing of your personal information for targeted advertising purposes under applicable state privacy laws, including the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA).", "We don't sell or share personal information, so nothing more needs to change for you. For more information about GPC and how to enable it, visit globalprivacycontrol.org."]}, {"heading": "13. Do United States residents have specific privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: If you are a resident of California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Florida, Indiana, Iowa, Kentucky, Maryland, Minnesota, Montana, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Jersey, Oregon, Rhode Island, Tennessee, Texas, Utah, or Virginia, you may have the right to request access to and receive details about the personal information we maintain about you and how we have processed it, correct inaccuracies, get a copy of, or delete your personal information.", "You may also have the right to withdraw your consent to our processing of your personal information. These rights may be limited in some circumstances by applicable law. More information is provided below.", "Categories of Personal Information We Collect. These are the categories of personal information we have collected in the past twelve (12) months. For a full list of what we process, see sections 1 and 18.", "A. Identifiers (such as name, alias, online identifier, Internet Protocol address, email address, and account name): YES. Your email address, display name, account id, and IP address in our providers' logs.", "B. Personal information as defined in the California Customer Records statute (name, contact information, education, employment, employment history, and financial information): YES, your display name and email address only.", "C. Protected classification characteristics under state or federal law (gender, age, date of birth, race and ethnicity, national origin, marital status, and other demographic data): YES, your date of birth only, to confirm you're 18 or over.", "D. Commercial information (transaction information, purchase history, financial details, and payment information): YES. Your plan, billing day and payment history (amounts, dates and whether each payment succeeded), gift memberships you bought or redeemed (plan, months, price and when redeemed), with Stripe's ids for your customer record and subscription. Card details are held by Stripe, not us.", "E. Biometric information (fingerprints and voiceprints): NO.", "F. Internet or other similar network activity (browsing history, search history, online behavior, interest data, and interactions with our and other websites, applications, systems, and advertisements): YES, within LiteraryWorm only: your searches and reading activity on our Services. Not your activity on other websites.", "G. Geolocation data (device location): NO.", "H. Audio, electronic, sensory, or similar information (images and audio, video or call recordings): NO.", "I. Professional or employment-related information: NO.", "J. Education Information (student records and directory information): NO.", "K. Inferences drawn from collected personal information (a profile or summary of an individual's preferences and characteristics): YES. Book recommendations, worked out in your browser from your own reading and not used for advertising.", "L. Sensitive personal information: YES, account login information only. No biometric data.", "We will use and retain the personal information in each category for as long as the user has an account with us.", "We only collect sensitive personal information, as defined by applicable privacy laws or the purposes allowed by law or with your consent. Sensitive personal information may be used, or disclosed to a service provider or contractor, for additional, specified purposes. You may have the right to limit the use or disclosure of your sensitive personal information. We do not collect or process sensitive personal information for the purpose of inferring characteristics about you.", "We may also collect other personal information outside of these categories when you receive help through our customer support channels, or to deliver our Services and respond to your inquiries.", "Sources of Personal Information. See section 1. How We Use and Share Personal Information. See sections 2 and 4.", "Will your information be shared with anyone else? We may disclose your personal information with our service providers pursuant to a written contract between us and each service provider. See section 4.", "We have not disclosed, sold, or shared any personal information to third parties for a business or commercial purpose in the preceding twelve (12) months. We will not sell or share personal information in the future belonging to website visitors, users, and other consumers.", "Your Rights. You have rights under certain US state data protection laws. However, these rights are not absolute, and in certain cases, we may decline your request as permitted by law.", "These rights include:", "the right to know whether or not we are processing your personal data;", "to access your personal data;", "to correct inaccuracies in your personal data;", "to request the deletion of your personal data;", "to obtain a copy of the personal data you previously shared with us;", "to non-discrimination for exercising your rights;", "and to opt out of the processing of your personal data if it is used for targeted advertising (or sharing as defined under California's privacy law), the sale of personal data, or profiling in furtherance of decisions that produce legal or similarly significant effects (\"profiling\").", "Depending upon the state where you live, you may also have the right:", "to access the categories of personal data being processed (including in Minnesota);", "to obtain a list of the categories of third parties to which we have disclosed personal data (including in California, Delaware, and Maryland);", "to obtain a list of specific third parties to which we have disclosed personal data (including in Minnesota and Oregon);", "to obtain a list of third parties to which we have sold personal data (including in Connecticut);", "to review, understand, question, and depending on where you live, correct how personal data has been profiled (including in Connecticut and Minnesota);", "to limit use and disclosure of sensitive personal data (including in California);", "and to opt out of the collection of sensitive data and personal data collected through the operation of a voice or facial recognition feature (including in Florida).", "How to Exercise Your Rights. To exercise these rights, you can use Account (to download, change or delete your data), email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com, or use the contact details at the bottom of this document. We will honor your opt-out preferences if you enact the Global Privacy Control (GPC) opt-out signal on your browser.", "Under certain US state data protection laws, you can designate an authorized agent to make a request on your behalf. We may deny a request from an authorized agent that does not submit proof that they have been validly authorized to act on your behalf in accordance with applicable laws.", "Request Verification. Upon receiving your request, we will need to verify your identity to determine you are the same person about whom we have the information in our system. We will only use personal information provided in your request to verify your identity or authority to make the request.", "However, if we cannot verify your identity from the information already maintained by us, we may request that you provide additional information for the purposes of verifying your identity and for security or fraud-prevention purposes.", "If you submit the request through an authorized agent, we may need to collect additional information to verify your identity before processing your request and the agent will need to provide a written and signed permission from you to submit such request on your behalf.", "Appeals. Under certain US state data protection laws, if we decline to take action regarding your request, you may appeal our decision by emailing us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. We will inform you in writing of any action taken or not taken in response to the appeal, including a written explanation of the reasons for the decisions. If your appeal is denied, you may submit a complaint to your state attorney general.", "California \"Shine The Light\" Law. California Civil Code Section 1798.83, also known as the \"Shine The Light\" law, permits our users who are California residents to request and obtain from us, once a year and free of charge, information about categories of personal information (if any) we disclosed to third parties for direct marketing purposes and the names and addresses of all third parties with which we shared personal information in the immediately preceding calendar year.", "We don't disclose personal information to third parties for their direct marketing. If you are a California resident and would like to make such a request, please submit your request in writing to us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below."]}, {"heading": "14. Do other regions have specific privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: You may have additional rights based on the country you reside in.", "Australia and New Zealand. We collect and process your personal information under the obligations and conditions set by Australia's Privacy Act 1988 and New Zealand's Privacy Act 2020 (Privacy Act).", "This Privacy Notice satisfies the notice requirements defined in both Privacy Acts, in particular: what personal information we collect from you, from which sources, for which purposes, and other recipients of your personal information.", "If you do not wish to provide the personal information necessary to fulfill their applicable purpose, it may affect our ability to provide our services, in particular to offer you the products or services that you want, respond to or help with your requests, manage your account with us, and confirm your identity and protect your account.", "At any time, you have the right to request access to or correction of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in section 20.", "If you believe we are unlawfully processing your personal information, you have the right to submit a complaint about a breach of the Australian Privacy Principles to the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner and a breach of New Zealand's Privacy Principles to the Office of New Zealand Privacy Commissioner.", "Republic of South Africa. At any time, you have the right to request access to or correction of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in section 20.", "If you are unsatisfied with the manner in which we address any complaint with regard to our processing of personal information, you can contact the office of the regulator, the Information Regulator (South Africa). General enquiries: enquiries@inforegulator.org.za. Complaints (complete POPIA/PAIA form 5): PAIAComplaints@inforegulator.org.za and POPIAComplaints@inforegulator.org.za."]}, {"heading": "15. Licensing and selling your information", "paragraphs": ["We don't sell, rent or license your personal information, and we don't share it for advertising. We use it only to run LiteraryWorm for you.", "Content you post (reading logs and club posts) stays yours. The Terms of Service explain the limited licence you give us to show it where you posted it.", "We don't use your reading history, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models."]}, {"heading": "16. How long we keep each kind of information", "paragraphs": ["We keep your information while your account is open, and some of it for less time, as this list shows.", "Your account, household and reading: until you delete them. Deleting a profile deletes its data, including its uploaded books.", "Deleting your account deletes everything at once, including your uploads; public logs and club posts go with it. Copies in our providers' backups go when those backups expire.", "Counts of requests, kept to protect the service from abuse and keyed by a one-way hash of your account or network address: cleared within a day.", "A gift recipient's email address and the buyer's message: until the gift email has gone.", "The one-way fingerprint of an address you invited to a book club: 30 days. New books we find by authors you follow: 60 days.", "The record of each support lookup: a year. Your cookie choice, kept in your browser by Termly: up to 12 months.", "Notifications on a device: until you turn them off there or log out on it."]}, {"heading": "17. Your choices and rights", "paragraphs": ["In Account you can download a copy of your data, change your display name, choose which emails and notifications you get, and delete profiles or your whole account. The Your Privacy Choices page sums these up. You can remove any log, post, highlight or upload yourself.", "You can also use the request form linked at the bottom of every page (Do Not Sell or Share My Personal information; Limit the Use Of My Sensitive Personal Information), provided by Termly.", "We honour Global Privacy Control. Children's data is covered by the Children's Privacy Notice, and what we keep in your browser by the Cookie Policy."]}, {"heading": "18. What we collect", "paragraphs": ["Your account: your email address, display name, password, date of birth (to confirm you're 18 or over), when you accepted the Terms, and your plan.", "Your email choices: marketing emails, emails when a book you set Remind me on is out, weekly emails about new books from authors you follow, and book club meeting emails (reminders, and when a meeting moves or is cancelled; on until you turn them off).", "The monthly What's new email, the weekly Kids reading report and the daily email of new club posts are off until you turn them on.", "Your password is handled by our sign-in provider and kept only as a one-way hash; we never see it. If you add passkeys, our sign-in provider keeps each one's public key, its name and when it was added and last used; the private key stays on your device, browser or password manager, and your face or fingerprint never leaves your device.", "You can rename or remove passkeys in Account → Sign-in, and Download my data lists them. If you use two-step sign-in, we also note which passkeys were added after you entered your code, so logging in with one of them doesn't ask for the code again.", "If you turn on notifications on a device, we keep that browser's push address and keys, a name for the device (like Chrome on Windows) and when it last got a notification, until you turn them off there or log out on it. Grown-up and teen profiles can put catalog books on a family shelf for the household; we keep which books, which profile added each and when, until they're taken off or the account is deleted.", "In a book club, if you turn on Show my place to this club, its members see the chapter you've reached in the club's book, how far through it you are and when you last read; turning it off hides it again.", "If you share one of your highlights with a book club, its members see the passage, its chapter and colour, your display name and, if you choose, your note, until you stop sharing it, delete the highlight or leave the club.", "Your household: each profile's name, avatar, age rating, favourite genres, daily limit, weekly goal, bedtime and wake-up time (Kids profiles) and player settings, and whether it's set aside. A profile PIN is kept only as a one-way hash. For Kids profiles, we keep a record of the parent's consent, and the books a Kids profile asks a grown-up for, with the answer.", "Your reading: your library, reading progress, bookmarks, highlights and notes (with when each highlight is next due for review).", "What you keep about books: the words you save from the dictionary (with the sentence you met them in and when each is next due for review), your private reading journal (dated entries about a book that only your account sees), ratings, the moods and pace you tag books with, favourites and lists, and your Up next queue.", "Who and what you follow: the authors and narrators you follow (their pages show how many readers follow them, never who; and, for 60 days, the new books we find by the authors you follow), release reminders and recent searches.", "Your goals and time: reading goals, the household challenges your profiles start or join, time read and listened each day, and the time each profile spends with each book on each day and where in the book the day started and ended (for the book page's Your time with this book).", "Your plans and progress: reading plans (the day a profile chose to finish a book by, and where it was in the book when the plan started), your reading pace (words a minute, used to show the time left in a chapter), chapter quiz scores, and the books you finish.", "If you turn on your reader page (it's off until you do), the reading logs you've already made public are listed together on one public page under your display name, and other members can follow it; who follows whom is never shown, and turning the page off removes its followers. Exports of your highlights and notes are made in your browser and downloaded to your device; they're never uploaded.", "So are library exports for Goodreads and StoryGraph (a CSV of your books, shelves, star ratings, dates, and the moods and pace you tagged); we don't send them to either site. Audio clips you share are links to a stretch of a public-domain LibriVox recording, played from the Internet Archive; we don't record or keep them, or who shared them.", "Things you share or add: reading logs (public, with your display name), book club posts and whether you're coming to a club's meetings (seen by the club's members), lists you choose to share by link, and books you upload (private to the profile that added them; we keep their text only; if you start a book club around one, the club's members see its title and author, never its text).", "Devices: while you're signed in, a short label for each browser you use the app in (such as \"Chrome on Windows\") so you can send a book to another device. For each device that keeps books with Read offline, the same kind of label and when it was last used, so your plan's offline devices can be counted and you can free one.", "LiteraryWorm never asks for access to your local network (the other devices on your home Wi-Fi) and doesn't need it for anything, including Listen on another device and playing on a speaker or TV. If your browser asks, choose Block."]}, {"heading": "19. Do we make updates to this notice?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: Yes, we will update this notice as necessary to stay compliant with relevant laws.", "We may update this Privacy Notice from time to time. The updated version will be indicated by an updated \"Last updated\" date at the top of this Privacy Notice. If we make material changes to this Privacy Notice, we may notify you either by prominently posting a notice of such changes or by directly sending you a notification. We encourage you to review this Privacy Notice frequently to be informed of how we are protecting your information."]}, {"heading": "20. How can you contact us about this notice?", "paragraphs": ["If you have questions or comments about this notice, you may email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "21. How can you review, update, or delete the data we collect from you?", "paragraphs": ["Based on the applicable laws of your country or state of residence in the US, you may have the right to request access to the personal information we collect from you, details about how we have processed it, correct inaccuracies, or delete your personal information. You may also have the right to withdraw your consent to our processing of your personal information. These rights may be limited in some circumstances by applicable law.", "To review, update, or delete your personal information, go to Account, where you can download a copy of your data, change it, or delete it, or email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}]$lw$::jsonb, updated_at = now()
where slug = $lw$privacy$lw$ and sections = $lw$[{"heading": "Questions or concerns?", "paragraphs": ["Reading this Privacy Notice will help you understand your privacy rights and choices. We are responsible for making decisions about how your personal information is processed. If you do not agree with our policies and practices, please do not use our Services. If you still have any questions or concerns, please contact us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "Summary of key points", "paragraphs": ["This summary provides key points from our Privacy Notice; you can find out more details about any of these topics in the numbered sections below.", "What personal information do we process? When you visit, use, or navigate our Services, we may process personal information depending on how you interact with us and the Services, the choices you make, and the products and features you use. See section 1.", "Do we process any sensitive personal information? Some information may be considered \"special\" or \"sensitive\" in certain jurisdictions, for example your racial or ethnic origins, sexual orientation, religious beliefs, biometric or financial data. We don't collect any of those. The only information we hold that some laws treat as sensitive is your account login information.", "Do we collect any information from third parties? Only from Stripe, which tells us whether your payments succeed and how your membership stands. We don't buy information about you or get it from social networks or data brokers.", "How do we process your information? We process your information to provide, improve, and administer our Services, communicate with you, for security and fraud prevention, and to comply with law. We may also process your information for other purposes with your consent. We process your information only when we have a valid legal reason to do so. See section 2.", "In what situations and with which parties do we share personal information? Only with the service providers that help us run LiteraryWorm, and in the specific situations described in section 4. We don't sell your personal information or share it for advertising.", "What don't we do? We don't sell your information or share it for advertising, we don't show ads, we don't use analytics trackers, and we don't use your reading, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models.", "How do we keep your information safe? Connections use HTTPS, our database lets each account read only its own data, passwords and PINs are kept only as one-way hashes, and you can add two-step sign-in or passkeys.", "However, no electronic transmission over the internet or information storage technology can be guaranteed to be 100% secure, so we cannot promise or guarantee that hackers, cybercriminals, or other unauthorized third parties will not be able to defeat our security and improperly collect, access, steal, or modify your information. See section 9.", "What are your rights? Depending on where you are located geographically, the applicable privacy law may mean you have certain rights regarding your personal information. See section 11.", "How do you exercise your rights? The easiest way is in Account, where you can download a copy of your data, change it, or delete it, or by contacting us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. We will consider and act upon any request in accordance with applicable data protection laws."]}, {"heading": "1. What information do we collect?", "paragraphs": ["Personal information you disclose to us. In Short: We collect personal information that you provide to us.", "We collect personal information that you voluntarily provide to us when you register on the Services, when you participate in activities on the Services, or otherwise when you contact us.", "Personal Information Provided by You. The personal information that we collect depends on the context of your interactions with us and the Services, the choices you make, and the products and features you use. It includes your email address, display name, password, date of birth, contact preferences (marketing emails) and authentication data, and what you read, save and share in the app.", "Section 18 lists exactly what we keep.", "Sensitive Information. We do not collect biometric data, financial data, or other special categories of personal information.", "Payment Data. Payments are handled by Stripe (https://stripe.com/privacy), which collects and stores your card details; we never see or store your full card number. We keep the plan you chose, your billing day, Stripe's ids for your customer record and subscription, and whether each payment succeeded.", "If you buy a gift membership, we keep its code, plan, number of months and price, and whether and when it was redeemed (and, for a gift that starts on a later day, when we reminded the buyer).", "A gift can come with up to 10 LiteraryWorm books the buyer picks: we keep which ones with the code, Stripe receives their ids with the payment, the email we send the recipient names them, and redeeming the gift adds them to the redeemer's library.", "If you ask us to email a gift to someone, Stripe receives their email address and your message with the payment, and we keep both only until the email has gone; only the buyer can see the code, and we record which account redeemed it.", "Social Media Login Data. We don't offer registration or log in with social media accounts.", "All personal information that you provide to us must be true, complete, and accurate, and you must notify us of any changes to such personal information.", "Information automatically collected. In Short: Some information — such as your Internet Protocol (IP) address and browser and device characteristics — is collected automatically when you visit our Services.", "Log and Usage Data. Our hosting and backend providers (Vercel and Supabase) automatically record service-related, diagnostic and performance information when you access the Services, in log files. This may include your IP address, browser type, the date and time of requests, the pages and files requested, and error reports. It is used to keep the Services secure and working, not for advertising, and we don't build profiles from it.", "Location Data. We don't collect your device's location, and we don't use GPS or similar technologies.", "Google API. We use the Google Books API only for book information: our server sends it search words or an ISBN, book ids and the names of authors people follow, never who's asking. Our use of information received from Google APIs will adhere to Google API Services User Data Policy, including the Limited Use requirements."]}, {"heading": "2. How do we process your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We process your information to provide, improve, and administer our Services, communicate with you, for security and fraud prevention, and to comply with law. We may also process your information for other purposes only with your prior explicit consent.", "To facilitate account creation and authentication and otherwise manage user accounts. We may process your information so you can create and log in to your account, as well as keep your account and household profiles in working order.", "To deliver and facilitate delivery of services to the user. We may process your information to provide you with the requested service, such as keeping your place in a book across devices, your bookmarks, highlights, journal, saved words, lists, Up next queue, goals and stats.", "To respond to user inquiries/offer support to users. We may process your information to respond to your inquiries and solve any potential issues you might have with the requested service.", "To send administrative information to you. We may process your information to send you details about your account, changes to our terms and policies, and other similar information.", "To enable user-to-user communications. We may process your information if you choose to use any of our offerings that allow for communication with another user, such as reading logs, book clubs and shared lists. A book club's calendar link lets your calendar app read the club's meetings (their names, times and notes) from us; the link is yours, we don't store it, and it stops working if you leave the club.", "To send you marketing and promotional communications, only if you turned on marketing emails. You can turn them off at any time in Account. For more information, see section 11.", "To show you recommendations. Book recommendations and reading stats are worked out in your browser from your own reading; they aren't used for advertising.", "To protect our Services. We may process your information as part of our efforts to keep our Services safe and secure, including fraud monitoring and prevention and limiting how often our features can be used.", "To save or protect an individual's vital interest. We may process your information when necessary to save or protect an individual's vital interest, such as to prevent harm.", "We don't process your information to deliver targeted advertising."]}, {"heading": "3. What legal bases do we rely on to process your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We only process your personal information when we believe it is necessary and we have a valid legal reason (i.e., legal basis) to do so under applicable law, like with your consent, to comply with laws, to provide you with services to enter into or fulfill our contractual obligations, to protect your rights, or to fulfill our legitimate business interests.", "If you are located in the EU or UK, this section applies to you. The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and UK GDPR require us to explain the valid legal bases we rely on in order to process your personal information. As such, we may rely on the following legal bases to process your personal information:", "Consent. We may process your information if you have given us permission (i.e., consent) to use your personal information for a specific purpose, such as marketing emails or a Kids profile. You can withdraw your consent at any time (see section 11).", "Performance of a Contract. We may process your personal information when we believe it is necessary to fulfill our contractual obligations to you, including providing our Services or at your request prior to entering into a contract with you.", "Legitimate Interests. We may process your information when we believe it is reasonably necessary to achieve our legitimate business interests and those interests do not outweigh your interests and fundamental rights and freedoms: to diagnose problems and prevent fraudulent activities, and to keep the Services secure and working.", "Legal Obligations. We may process your information where we believe it is necessary for compliance with our legal obligations, such as to cooperate with a law enforcement body or regulatory agency, exercise or defend our legal rights, or disclose your information as evidence in litigation in which we are involved.", "Vital Interests. We may process your information where we believe it is necessary to protect your vital interests or the vital interests of a third party, such as situations involving potential threats to the safety of any person.", "If you are located in Canada, this section applies to you. We may process your information if you have given us specific permission (i.e., express consent) to use your personal information for a specific purpose, or in situations where your permission can be inferred (i.e., implied consent). You can withdraw your consent at any time.", "In some exceptional cases, we may be legally permitted under applicable law to process your information without your consent, including, for example:", "if collection is clearly in the interests of an individual and consent cannot be obtained in a timely way;", "for investigations and fraud detection and prevention;", "for business transactions provided certain conditions are met;", "if it is contained in a witness statement and the collection is necessary to assess, process, or settle an insurance claim;", "for identifying injured, ill, or deceased persons and communicating with next of kin;", "if we have reasonable grounds to believe an individual has been, is, or may be victim of financial abuse;", "if it is reasonable to expect collection and use with consent would compromise the availability or the accuracy of the information and the collection is reasonable for purposes related to investigating a breach of an agreement or a contravention of the laws of Canada or a province;", "if disclosure is required to comply with a subpoena, warrant, court order, or rules of the court relating to the production of records;", "if it was produced by an individual in the course of their employment, business, or profession and the collection is consistent with the purposes for which the information was produced;", "if the collection is solely for journalistic, artistic, or literary purposes;", "and if the information is publicly available and is specified by the regulations.", "We may disclose de-identified information for approved research or statistics projects, subject to ethics oversight and confidentiality commitments."]}, {"heading": "4. When and with whom do we share your personal information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We share information only with the service providers that help us run LiteraryWorm, and in the situations described in this section.", "Service providers. Supabase stores the data above and runs our server functions. Vercel hosts the website.", "Mailgun sends our emails and the book club invitations members send, so it receives the address each email goes to and the email's contents.", "The emails confirm your email address, reset your password, and tell you when a book you set Remind me on is out. There's a weekly list of new books from authors you follow, and a monthly round-up of what's new in LiteraryWorm if you turn it on.", "If you turn it on, a weekly report of how your Kids profiles read: their names, reading and listening time, days read, books finished and any weekly goal.", "A reminder the day before a book club you're in meets, and a note when one of its meetings moves or is cancelled: the club's name and book and the meeting's name, times and note, with the meeting as a calendar file.", "If you turn it on, a daily email of new posts in your book clubs: each club's name and book, the chapters you've reached that have new posts, how many and the display names of who wrote them; never what they wrote.", "A reminder three days before a gift membership you bought for a later day starts (its plan, months and start day; never the code), and a gift membership a member asked us to email to you (its code, plan and months, the buyer's display name and any message they wrote).", "And an email confirming that your account was deleted.", "We turn off Mailgun's open and click tracking.", "Our service providers may use your information only to provide their service to us, except where this section says they handle it under their own policy.", "If you turn on notifications on a device, the notification about a book you set Remind me on (its title and author, and the profile's name) goes through your browser's push service (Google for Chrome and Android, Apple for Safari, Mozilla for Firefox, Microsoft for Edge), encrypted so that only your browser can read it.", "You choose in Account → Preferences which kinds your devices get; besides release days they can be a club meeting in the next day (the club's and meeting's names), new posts in your clubs (the club's name, how many, the chapter and who wrote them; never what they said), an evening reading-streak nudge (a profile's name and run of days; for this we keep each device's time zone) and a grown-up's answer to a Kids book request (the profile's name and the book).", "Stripe processes payments. When you add a payment method, Stripe receives your name, email address, card details and billing address, and handles them under its own privacy notice (https://stripe.com/privacy).", "Termly provides our cookie consent banner and our privacy request forms. Your browser loads the banner from Termly on each visit, so Termly receives those requests, including your IP address, and your consent choice is kept in your browser. Termly handles this under its own privacy policy (https://termly.io/our-privacy-policy/). It never handles payment details: Stripe does.", "Anthropic (Claude) writes book summaries and content notes, answers Ask the book questions, translates passages you select, writes Who's who and writes chapter quizzes and Previously… recaps (see section 6).", "Google Books answers searches and book details through our server, which sends only the search words or ISBN, book ids and the names of authors people follow, never who's asking. Book covers and the Google Books preview load from books.google.com directly in your browser, and Google sets third-party cookies there (see the Cookie Policy).", "LibriVox recordings stream from the Internet Archive directly from your browser. The app's fonts load from Google Fonts the same way, so Google receives your IP address with those requests.", "Other members. Reading logs are public, with your display name. Book club posts are seen by the club's members. If you invite someone to a club by email, the invitation shows them your display name and the club's name, book and finish date; we send it through Mailgun and keep only a one-way fingerprint of their address for 30 days (so the same address isn't invited again within a week), never the address itself.", "Lists you share can be read by anyone with the link. Staff with the right access can see account details to run the service and help you.", "Our support team. To help with a question, LiteraryWorm staff with the customer service role (and admins) can look up an account by its email address.", "They see the name and email, when the account was made, whether the email is confirmed and two-step sign-in is on, the plan and its billing state (never card details), how many profiles there are, this month's listening against the plan, and the devices keeping books offline. They never see your library, reading, notes, highlights or searches. Every lookup is recorded with who made it and when, and the record is deleted after a year.", "Business Transfers. We may share or transfer your information in connection with, or during negotiations of, any merger, sale of company assets, financing, or acquisition of all or a portion of our business to another company."]}, {"heading": "5. Do we use cookies and other tracking technologies?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We use only essential cookies and similar technologies, described in our Cookie Policy. Because we use the Google Books API, Google sets third-party cookies from books.google.com when book covers and previews load.", "When you first visit, a banner from Termly asks for your cookie choice, and you can change it at any time with Consent Preferences at the bottom of every page. Because we use only essential cookies, declining changes nothing about how the Services work.", "The technologies we use help us maintain the security of our Services and your account, keep you signed in, and let the app work offline.", "We don't permit third parties or service providers to use online tracking technologies on our Services for analytics or advertising, and we don't use Google Analytics or any other analytics or advertising service.", "Google Books covers and the Google Books preview load from books.google.com, and Google sets its own third-party cookies there, under Google's Privacy Policy. We don't receive them. To stop them, block third-party cookies in your browser; LiteraryWorm keeps working.", "Specific information about the technologies we use and how you can refuse certain cookies is set out in our Cookie Policy."]}, {"heading": "6. Do we offer artificial intelligence-based products?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We offer products, features, or tools powered by artificial intelligence, machine learning, or similar technologies.", "As part of our Services, we offer features powered by artificial intelligence (\"AI Products\"). The terms in this Privacy Notice govern your use of the AI Products within our Services.", "Use of AI Technologies. We provide the AI Products through one third-party service provider (\"AI Service Provider\"): Anthropic (Claude). We don't use OpenAI, Hugging Face, Google Cloud AI, DeepSeek or Gemini to process your information. Figma Make was used only to design the app's screens and never receives your information. You must not use the AI Products in any way that violates the terms or policies of the AI Service Provider.", "Our AI Products. Book summaries: short and detailed summaries of books in our catalog, written the first time anyone asks, from the book's details; no personal information is sent.", "Content notes: for each of our titles, how much violence, death, frightening scenes, language, sexual content, alcohol and drugs, prejudice and self-harm it has, written the first time anyone asks, from the book's details; no personal information is sent, and the notes are kept so everyone sees the same ones.", "Ask the book: answers to your questions about a public-domain book. We send your question and the book's text up to your chapter; we don't send your name, email or account details.", "Translate: a passage you select in a public-domain book, in another language. We send only the words you selected (they must be in that chapter), nothing about you, and keep nothing.", "Who's who: the characters you've met in a public-domain book so far. We send only the book's text up to your chapter, nothing about you, and keep the list so the next reader at that chapter gets the same one.", "Previously…: in the player, a recap of a public-domain book up to the chapter you've reached. We send only the text of the chapters before it, nothing about you, and keep the recap so the next listener there gets the same one.", "Chapter quizzes: a few questions about a chapter of a public-domain book, written for young readers. We send only that chapter's text, nothing about you, and keep the quiz so every reader of that chapter gets the same one; your score is saved with your profile.", "Ask the book, Translate and Who's who aren't offered to Kids profiles; summaries, content notes, recaps and quizzes are. Summaries, content notes, answers, translations, Who's who, recaps and quizzes are labelled as written by AI.", "How We Process Your Data Using AI. All personal information processed using our AI Products is handled in line with this Privacy Notice and our agreement with the AI Service Provider. We don't use your reading history, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models."]}, {"heading": "7. How do we handle your social logins?", "paragraphs": ["We don't offer registration or log in using social media accounts. If we add it, we'll update this Privacy Notice first."]}, {"heading": "8. How long do we keep your information?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We keep your information for as long as necessary to fulfill the purposes outlined in this Privacy Notice unless otherwise required by law.", "We will only keep your personal information for as long as it is necessary for the purposes set out in this Privacy Notice, unless a longer retention period is required or permitted by law (such as tax, accounting, or other legal requirements). No purpose in this notice will require us keeping your personal information for longer than the period of time in which users have an account with us. Section 16 lists how long we keep each kind of information.", "When we have no ongoing legitimate business need to process your personal information, we will either delete or anonymize such information, or, if this is not possible (for example, because your personal information has been stored in backup archives), then we will securely store your personal information and isolate it from any further processing until deletion is possible."]}, {"heading": "9. How do we keep your information safe?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We aim to protect your personal information through a system of organizational and technical security measures.", "We have implemented appropriate and reasonable technical and organizational security measures designed to protect the security of any personal information we process. For example, connections use HTTPS, and access rules in our database let each account read only its own data.", "Passwords and profile PINs are kept only as one-way hashes. You can turn on two-step sign-in or log in with a passkey, staff can see only what their role needs, and every support lookup is recorded.", "However, despite our safeguards and efforts to secure your information, no electronic transmission over the Internet or information storage technology can be guaranteed to be 100% secure, so we cannot promise or guarantee that hackers, cybercriminals, or other unauthorized third parties will not be able to defeat our security and improperly collect, access, steal, or modify your information.", "Although we will do our best to protect your personal information, transmission of personal information to and from our Services is at your own risk. You should only access the Services within a secure environment."]}, {"heading": "10. Do we collect information from minors?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: We do not knowingly collect data from or market to children under 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction, except through Kids profiles that a parent or guardian creates on their own account.", "Account holders must be 18 or older. We do not knowingly collect, solicit data from, or market to children under 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction, nor do we knowingly sell such personal information.", "By using the Services, you represent that you are at least 18 or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction or that you are the parent or guardian of such a minor and consent to such minor dependent's use of the Services through a Kids profile. The Children's Privacy Notice explains what Kids profiles keep.", "If we learn that personal information from users less than 18 years of age or the equivalent age as specified by law in your jurisdiction has been collected without that consent, we will deactivate the account and take reasonable measures to promptly delete such data from our records. If you become aware of any such data, please contact us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "11. What are your privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: Depending on your state of residence in the US or in some regions, such as the European Economic Area (EEA), United Kingdom (UK), Switzerland, and Canada, you have rights that allow you greater access to and control over your personal information. You may review, change, or terminate your account at any time, depending on your country, province, or state of residence.", "In some regions (like the EEA, UK, Switzerland, and Canada), you have certain rights under applicable data protection laws.", "These may include the right (i) to request access and obtain a copy of your personal information, (ii) to request rectification or erasure; (iii) to restrict the processing of your personal information; (iv) if applicable, to data portability; and (v) not to be subject to automated decision-making.", "If a decision that produces legal or similarly significant effects is made solely by automated means, we will inform you, explain the main factors, and offer a simple way to request human review. In certain circumstances, you may also have the right to object to the processing of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below.", "We will consider and act upon any request in accordance with applicable data protection laws.", "If you are located in the UK and are unhappy with how we have handled your personal information, you can make a complaint directly to us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. This is in addition to the rights you have under the UK General Data Protection Regulation and the Data Protection Act 2018.", "What happens after you complain: we will acknowledge your complaint within 30 days of receiving it, investigate without unjustifiable or excessive delay, keep you informed of progress and explain the outcome.", "If you are not happy with our final response, you can refer your complaint to the Information Commissioner's Office, the UK supervisory authority. Website: ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint. Helpline: 0303 123 1113. Post: Information Commissioner's Office, Wycliffe House, Water Lane, Wilmslow, Cheshire, SK9 5AF.", "If you are located in the EEA or UK and you believe we are unlawfully processing your personal information, you also have the right to complain to your Member State data protection authority or UK data protection authority.", "If you are located in Switzerland, you may contact the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner.", "Withdrawing your consent: If we are relying on your consent to process your personal information, which may be express and/or implied consent depending on the applicable law, you have the right to withdraw your consent at any time. You can withdraw your consent at any time in Account (for marketing emails and Kids profiles) or by contacting us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below.", "However, please note that this will not affect the lawfulness of the processing before its withdrawal nor, when applicable law allows, will it affect the processing of your personal information conducted in reliance on lawful processing grounds other than consent.", "Opting out of marketing and promotional communications: You can unsubscribe from our marketing and promotional communications at any time by turning off marketing emails in Account, by clicking on the unsubscribe link in the emails that we send, or by contacting us.", "We may still communicate with you, for example to send you service-related messages that are necessary for the administration and use of your account (each kind of email can be turned off in Account → Preferences, and the monthly What's new email, the weekly Kids reading report, book club meeting emails and club post emails also have a one-click unsubscribe link that works without signing in), to respond to service requests, or for other non-marketing purposes.", "Account Information. If you would at any time like to review or change the information in your account or terminate your account, you can log in and go to Account. There you can change your details, download a copy of your data, and delete profiles or your whole account. Deleting your account deletes its information from our databases straight away; copies in our providers' backups are removed when those backups expire.", "Cookies and similar technologies: we use only essential cookies, so declining cookies in Consent Preferences changes nothing about how the Services work. Blocking third-party cookies in your browser stops Google's cookies, and LiteraryWorm keeps working.", "Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out and removes books saved for offline. The Cookie Policy explains each one.", "If you have questions or comments about your privacy rights, you may email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "12. Controls for Do-Not-Track features", "paragraphs": ["Most web browsers and some mobile operating systems and mobile applications include a Do-Not-Track (\"DNT\") feature or setting you can activate to signal your privacy preference not to have data about your online browsing activities monitored and collected. At this stage, no uniform technology standard for recognizing and implementing DNT signals has been finalized.", "As such, we do not currently respond to DNT browser signals. We do honour Global Privacy Control, described below. We don't track you across other websites in any case. If a standard for online tracking is adopted that we must follow in the future, we will inform you about that practice in a revised version of this Privacy Notice.", "California law requires us to let you know how we respond to web browser DNT signals. Because there currently is not an industry or legal standard for recognizing or honoring DNT signals, we do not respond to them at this time.", "Global Privacy Control: We recognize and honor Global Privacy Control (GPC) signals. If you use a browser or extension that supports GPC, we will treat this as a valid request to opt out of the sale or sharing of your personal information for targeted advertising purposes under applicable state privacy laws, including the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA).", "We don't sell or share personal information, so nothing more needs to change for you. For more information about GPC and how to enable it, visit globalprivacycontrol.org."]}, {"heading": "13. Do United States residents have specific privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: If you are a resident of California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Florida, Indiana, Iowa, Kentucky, Maryland, Minnesota, Montana, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Jersey, Oregon, Rhode Island, Tennessee, Texas, Utah, or Virginia, you may have the right to request access to and receive details about the personal information we maintain about you and how we have processed it, correct inaccuracies, get a copy of, or delete your personal information.", "You may also have the right to withdraw your consent to our processing of your personal information. These rights may be limited in some circumstances by applicable law. More information is provided below.", "Categories of Personal Information We Collect. These are the categories of personal information we have collected in the past twelve (12) months. For a full list of what we process, see sections 1 and 18.", "A. Identifiers (such as name, alias, online identifier, Internet Protocol address, email address, and account name): YES. Your email address, display name, account id, and IP address in our providers' logs.", "B. Personal information as defined in the California Customer Records statute (name, contact information, education, employment, employment history, and financial information): YES, your display name and email address only.", "C. Protected classification characteristics under state or federal law (gender, age, date of birth, race and ethnicity, national origin, marital status, and other demographic data): YES, your date of birth only, to confirm you're 18 or over.", "D. Commercial information (transaction information, purchase history, financial details, and payment information): YES. Your plan, billing day and payment history (amounts, dates and whether each payment succeeded), gift memberships you bought or redeemed (plan, months, price and when redeemed), with Stripe's ids for your customer record and subscription. Card details are held by Stripe, not us.", "E. Biometric information (fingerprints and voiceprints): NO.", "F. Internet or other similar network activity (browsing history, search history, online behavior, interest data, and interactions with our and other websites, applications, systems, and advertisements): YES, within LiteraryWorm only: your searches and reading activity on our Services. Not your activity on other websites.", "G. Geolocation data (device location): NO.", "H. Audio, electronic, sensory, or similar information (images and audio, video or call recordings): NO.", "I. Professional or employment-related information: NO.", "J. Education Information (student records and directory information): NO.", "K. Inferences drawn from collected personal information (a profile or summary of an individual's preferences and characteristics): YES. Book recommendations, worked out in your browser from your own reading and not used for advertising.", "L. Sensitive personal information: YES, account login information only. No biometric data.", "We will use and retain the personal information in each category for as long as the user has an account with us.", "We only collect sensitive personal information, as defined by applicable privacy laws or the purposes allowed by law or with your consent. Sensitive personal information may be used, or disclosed to a service provider or contractor, for additional, specified purposes. You may have the right to limit the use or disclosure of your sensitive personal information. We do not collect or process sensitive personal information for the purpose of inferring characteristics about you.", "We may also collect other personal information outside of these categories when you receive help through our customer support channels, or to deliver our Services and respond to your inquiries.", "Sources of Personal Information. See section 1. How We Use and Share Personal Information. See sections 2 and 4.", "Will your information be shared with anyone else? We may disclose your personal information with our service providers pursuant to a written contract between us and each service provider. See section 4.", "We have not disclosed, sold, or shared any personal information to third parties for a business or commercial purpose in the preceding twelve (12) months. We will not sell or share personal information in the future belonging to website visitors, users, and other consumers.", "Your Rights. You have rights under certain US state data protection laws. However, these rights are not absolute, and in certain cases, we may decline your request as permitted by law.", "These rights include:", "the right to know whether or not we are processing your personal data;", "to access your personal data;", "to correct inaccuracies in your personal data;", "to request the deletion of your personal data;", "to obtain a copy of the personal data you previously shared with us;", "to non-discrimination for exercising your rights;", "and to opt out of the processing of your personal data if it is used for targeted advertising (or sharing as defined under California's privacy law), the sale of personal data, or profiling in furtherance of decisions that produce legal or similarly significant effects (\"profiling\").", "Depending upon the state where you live, you may also have the right:", "to access the categories of personal data being processed (including in Minnesota);", "to obtain a list of the categories of third parties to which we have disclosed personal data (including in California, Delaware, and Maryland);", "to obtain a list of specific third parties to which we have disclosed personal data (including in Minnesota and Oregon);", "to obtain a list of third parties to which we have sold personal data (including in Connecticut);", "to review, understand, question, and depending on where you live, correct how personal data has been profiled (including in Connecticut and Minnesota);", "to limit use and disclosure of sensitive personal data (including in California);", "and to opt out of the collection of sensitive data and personal data collected through the operation of a voice or facial recognition feature (including in Florida).", "How to Exercise Your Rights. To exercise these rights, you can use Account (to download, change or delete your data), email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com, or use the contact details at the bottom of this document. We will honor your opt-out preferences if you enact the Global Privacy Control (GPC) opt-out signal on your browser.", "Under certain US state data protection laws, you can designate an authorized agent to make a request on your behalf. We may deny a request from an authorized agent that does not submit proof that they have been validly authorized to act on your behalf in accordance with applicable laws.", "Request Verification. Upon receiving your request, we will need to verify your identity to determine you are the same person about whom we have the information in our system. We will only use personal information provided in your request to verify your identity or authority to make the request.", "However, if we cannot verify your identity from the information already maintained by us, we may request that you provide additional information for the purposes of verifying your identity and for security or fraud-prevention purposes.", "If you submit the request through an authorized agent, we may need to collect additional information to verify your identity before processing your request and the agent will need to provide a written and signed permission from you to submit such request on your behalf.", "Appeals. Under certain US state data protection laws, if we decline to take action regarding your request, you may appeal our decision by emailing us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com. We will inform you in writing of any action taken or not taken in response to the appeal, including a written explanation of the reasons for the decisions. If your appeal is denied, you may submit a complaint to your state attorney general.", "California \"Shine The Light\" Law. California Civil Code Section 1798.83, also known as the \"Shine The Light\" law, permits our users who are California residents to request and obtain from us, once a year and free of charge, information about categories of personal information (if any) we disclosed to third parties for direct marketing purposes and the names and addresses of all third parties with which we shared personal information in the immediately preceding calendar year.", "We don't disclose personal information to third parties for their direct marketing. If you are a California resident and would like to make such a request, please submit your request in writing to us by using the contact details provided in the section \"20. How can you contact us about this notice?\" below."]}, {"heading": "14. Do other regions have specific privacy rights?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: You may have additional rights based on the country you reside in.", "Australia and New Zealand. We collect and process your personal information under the obligations and conditions set by Australia's Privacy Act 1988 and New Zealand's Privacy Act 2020 (Privacy Act).", "This Privacy Notice satisfies the notice requirements defined in both Privacy Acts, in particular: what personal information we collect from you, from which sources, for which purposes, and other recipients of your personal information.", "If you do not wish to provide the personal information necessary to fulfill their applicable purpose, it may affect our ability to provide our services, in particular to offer you the products or services that you want, respond to or help with your requests, manage your account with us, and confirm your identity and protect your account.", "At any time, you have the right to request access to or correction of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in section 20.", "If you believe we are unlawfully processing your personal information, you have the right to submit a complaint about a breach of the Australian Privacy Principles to the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner and a breach of New Zealand's Privacy Principles to the Office of New Zealand Privacy Commissioner.", "Republic of South Africa. At any time, you have the right to request access to or correction of your personal information. You can make such a request by contacting us by using the contact details provided in section 20.", "If you are unsatisfied with the manner in which we address any complaint with regard to our processing of personal information, you can contact the office of the regulator, the Information Regulator (South Africa). General enquiries: enquiries@inforegulator.org.za. Complaints (complete POPIA/PAIA form 5): PAIAComplaints@inforegulator.org.za and POPIAComplaints@inforegulator.org.za."]}, {"heading": "15. Licensing and selling your information", "paragraphs": ["We don't sell, rent or license your personal information, and we don't share it for advertising. We use it only to run LiteraryWorm for you.", "Content you post (reading logs and club posts) stays yours. The Terms of Service explain the limited licence you give us to show it where you posted it.", "We don't use your reading history, notes, uploads or posts to train AI models."]}, {"heading": "16. How long we keep each kind of information", "paragraphs": ["We keep your information while your account is open, and some of it for less time, as this list shows.", "Your account, household and reading: until you delete them. Deleting a profile deletes its data, including its uploaded books.", "Deleting your account deletes everything at once, including your uploads; public logs and club posts go with it. Copies in our providers' backups go when those backups expire.", "Counts of requests, kept to protect the service from abuse and keyed by a one-way hash of your account or network address: cleared within a day.", "A gift recipient's email address and the buyer's message: until the gift email has gone.", "The one-way fingerprint of an address you invited to a book club: 30 days. New books we find by authors you follow: 60 days.", "The record of each support lookup: a year. Your cookie choice, kept in your browser by Termly: up to 12 months.", "Notifications on a device: until you turn them off there or log out on it."]}, {"heading": "17. Your choices and rights", "paragraphs": ["In Account you can download a copy of your data, change your display name, choose which emails and notifications you get, and delete profiles or your whole account. The Your Privacy Choices page sums these up. You can remove any log, post, highlight or upload yourself.", "You can also use the request form linked at the bottom of every page (Do Not Sell or Share My Personal information; Limit the Use Of My Sensitive Personal Information), provided by Termly.", "We honour Global Privacy Control. Children's data is covered by the Children's Privacy Notice, and what we keep in your browser by the Cookie Policy."]}, {"heading": "18. What we collect", "paragraphs": ["Your account: your email address, display name, password, date of birth (to confirm you're 18 or over), when you accepted the Terms, and your plan.", "Your email choices: marketing emails, emails when a book you set Remind me on is out, weekly emails about new books from authors you follow, and book club meeting emails (reminders, and when a meeting moves or is cancelled; on until you turn them off).", "The monthly What's new email, the weekly Kids reading report and the daily email of new club posts are off until you turn them on.", "Your password is handled by our sign-in provider and kept only as a one-way hash; we never see it. If you add passkeys, our sign-in provider keeps each one's public key, its name and when it was added and last used; the private key stays on your device, browser or password manager, and your face or fingerprint never leaves your device.", "You can rename or remove passkeys in Account → Sign-in, and Download my data lists them. If you use two-step sign-in, we also note which passkeys were added after you entered your code, so logging in with one of them doesn't ask for the code again.", "If you turn on notifications on a device, we keep that browser's push address and keys, a name for the device (like Chrome on Windows) and when it last got a notification, until you turn them off there or log out on it. Grown-up and teen profiles can put catalog books on a family shelf for the household; we keep which books, which profile added each and when, until they're taken off or the account is deleted.", "In a book club, if you turn on Show my place to this club, its members see the chapter you've reached in the club's book, how far through it you are and when you last read; turning it off hides it again.", "If you share one of your highlights with a book club, its members see the passage, its chapter and colour, your display name and, if you choose, your note, until you stop sharing it, delete the highlight or leave the club.", "Your household: each profile's name, avatar, age rating, favourite genres, daily limit, weekly goal, bedtime and wake-up time (Kids profiles) and player settings, and whether it's set aside. A profile PIN is kept only as a one-way hash. For Kids profiles, we keep a record of the parent's consent, and the books a Kids profile asks a grown-up for, with the answer.", "Your reading: your library, reading progress, bookmarks, highlights and notes (with when each highlight is next due for review).", "What you keep about books: the words you save from the dictionary (with the sentence you met them in and when each is next due for review), your private reading journal (dated entries about a book that only your account sees), ratings, the moods and pace you tag books with, favourites and lists, and your Up next queue.", "Who and what you follow: the authors and narrators you follow (their pages show how many readers follow them, never who; and, for 60 days, the new books we find by the authors you follow), release reminders and recent searches.", "Your goals and time: reading goals, the household challenges your profiles start or join, time read and listened each day, and the time each profile spends with each book on each day and where in the book the day started and ended (for the book page's Your time with this book).", "Your plans and progress: reading plans (the day a profile chose to finish a book by, and where it was in the book when the plan started), your reading pace (words a minute, used to show the time left in a chapter), chapter quiz scores, and the books you finish.", "If you turn on your reader page (it's off until you do), the reading logs you've already made public are listed together on one public page under your display name, and other members can follow it; who follows whom is never shown, and turning the page off removes its followers. Exports of your highlights and notes are made in your browser and downloaded to your device; they're never uploaded.", "So are library exports for Goodreads and StoryGraph (a CSV of your books, shelves, star ratings, dates, and the moods and pace you tagged); we don't send them to either site. Audio clips you share are links to a stretch of a public-domain LibriVox recording, played from the Internet Archive; we don't record or keep them, or who shared them.", "Things you share or add: reading logs (public, with your display name), book club posts and whether you're coming to a club's meetings (seen by the club's members), lists you choose to share by link, and books you upload (private to the profile that added them; we keep their text only; if you start a book club around one, the club's members see its title and author, never its text).", "Devices: while you're signed in, a short label for each browser you use the app in (such as \"Chrome on Windows\") so you can send a book to another device. For each device that keeps books with Read offline, the same kind of label and when it was last used, so your plan's offline devices can be counted and you can free one.", "LiteraryWorm never asks for access to your local network (the other devices on your home Wi-Fi) and doesn't need it for anything, including Listen on another device and playing on a speaker or TV. If your browser asks, choose Block."]}, {"heading": "19. Do we make updates to this notice?", "paragraphs": ["In Short: Yes, we will update this notice as necessary to stay compliant with relevant laws.", "We may update this Privacy Notice from time to time. The updated version will be indicated by an updated \"Last updated\" date at the top of this Privacy Notice. If we make material changes to this Privacy Notice, we may notify you either by prominently posting a notice of such changes or by directly sending you a notification. We encourage you to review this Privacy Notice frequently to be informed of how we are protecting your information."]}, {"heading": "20. How can you contact us about this notice?", "paragraphs": ["If you have questions or comments about this notice, you may email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}, {"heading": "21. How can you review, update, or delete the data we collect from you?", "paragraphs": ["Based on the applicable laws of your country or state of residence in the US, you may have the right to request access to the personal information we collect from you, details about how we have processed it, correct inaccuracies, or delete your personal information. You may also have the right to withdraw your consent to our processing of your personal information. These rights may be limited in some circumstances by applicable law.", "To review, update, or delete your personal information, go to Account, where you can download a copy of your data, change it, or delete it, or email us at usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}]$lw$::jsonb;

update public.legal_pages set sections = $lw$[{"heading": "In short", "paragraphs": ["We use no advertising or analytics cookies, and we don't track you across other websites.", "If you allow analytics in the consent banner, Ahrefs Web Analytics counts the pages you visit without setting cookies. It never runs for Kids profiles.", "Two kinds of cookie are set when you use LiteraryWorm, both by other companies: Cloudflare's bot check on our backend, and Google's cookies from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load.", "Your browser also keeps your sign-in, which device it is, your cookie choice, the app's own files and any books you save for offline. The list below says what each one is and how long it stays."]}, {"heading": "About this policy", "paragraphs": ["This Cookie Policy covers cookies and the similar things your browser keeps for LiteraryWorm, such as local storage and the app's offline caches. The Privacy Policy explains how we use the information itself.", "Some of what your browser keeps, such as your sign-in session, is linked to your account, so we treat it as personal information."]}, {"heading": "What are cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Cookies are small data files that are placed on your computer or mobile device when you visit a website. Cookies are widely used by website owners in order to make their websites work, or to work more efficiently, as well as to provide reporting information.", "Cookies set by the website owner (in this case, LiteraryWorm) are called \"first-party cookies.\" Cookies set by parties other than the website owner are called \"third-party cookies.\" Third-party cookies enable third-party features or functionality to be provided on or through the website (e.g., advertising, interactive content, and analytics).", "The parties that set these third-party cookies can recognize your computer both when it visits the website in question and also when it visits certain other websites."]}, {"heading": "Everything at a glance", "paragraphs": ["Here is everything that's set or kept in your browser when you use LiteraryWorm, who sets it and how long it stays. Each is explained further down.", "__cf_bm (a cookie, set by Cloudflare on .supabase.co, our backend): keeps out bots. Stays 30 minutes.", "Google's cookies (set by Google on books.google.com): set when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API. Google decides how long they stay; we can't read them.", "literaryworm-session (local storage): keeps you signed in. Stays until you log out.", "literaryworm-device (local storage): which of your devices this browser is, as a random id and your account id. Stays until you log out.", "TERMLY_API_CACHE (local storage, kept by Termly): your cookie choice. Stays up to 12 months.", "The app's files (the lw-shell cache): so the app opens quickly and offline. Replaced when the app updates.", "Books saved for offline (the lw-books cache): stay until you remove them, with a small note of your offline place and any listening schedule.", "Books you uploaded and opened, or searched by keywords (the lw-uploads cache): stay until you delete the book or log out.", "A push subscription, only if you turn on notifications on this device: stays until you turn them off or log out."]}, {"heading": "Why do we use cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Some cookies are required for technical reasons in order for our Website to operate, and we refer to these as \"essential\" or \"strictly necessary\" cookies. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies and similar technologies, including the third-party cookies Google sets from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API.", "We don't use cookies to track or target your interests, and we don't let third parties serve cookies through our Website for advertising or analytics. This is described in more detail below."]}, {"heading": "How can I control cookies?", "paragraphs": ["You have the right to decide whether to accept or reject cookies. Essential cookies cannot be rejected as they are strictly necessary to provide you with services.", "When you first visit, a banner from Termly, our consent provider, asks for your choice, and you can change it at any time with Consent Preferences at the bottom of every page. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies, so declining changes nothing about how the site works: book covers, audiobooks and your account work either way. If we ever add cookies that aren't essential, they'll only be used with your consent, and we'll update this page.", "You may also set or amend your web browser controls to accept or refuse cookies. Blocking third-party cookies stops Google's cookies, and LiteraryWorm keeps working. Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out and removes books saved for offline.", "The cookies set when you use our Website, and what each one does, are described below. Which ones are set can depend on the pages you visit: Google's, for example, only when a Google Books cover or preview loads."]}, {"heading": "Essential website cookies", "paragraphs": ["These cookies are strictly necessary to provide you with services available through our Website and to use some of its features, such as access to secure areas.", "__cf_bm. Purpose: Cloudflare places the cookie on end-user devices that access customer sites protected by Bot Management or Bot Fight Mode. Provider: .supabase.co (our backend, Supabase, which is protected by Cloudflare). Service: Cloudflare (see Cloudflare's privacy policy). Type: server cookie. Expires in: 30 minutes.", "Google Books cookies. Purpose: LiteraryWorm uses the Google Books API to find books and show their details. So the covers of books from Google Books, and the Google Books preview on some book pages (only when you open it), load in your browser from books.google.com, and Google sets and reads its own third-party cookies there.", "Provider: books.google.com (Google). Service: Google Books (see Google's Privacy Policy at policies.google.com/privacy). Type: third-party cookies, set by Google, not by LiteraryWorm; we can't read them.", "We mark Google Books covers and the preview as essential, so they show even if you decline other cookies in Consent Preferences. To stop Google's cookies, block third-party cookies in your browser's settings: LiteraryWorm keeps working, and covers still show. Kids profiles show only LiteraryWorm's own titles, so they don't load Google Books.", "The audiobook recordings streamed from the Internet Archive load from that service too, and it may set or read its own cookies under its own policy."]}, {"heading": "Similar technologies: what we keep in your browser", "paragraphs": ["Signed in: when you log in, your browser keeps your sign-in session (in local storage, under literaryworm-session) so you stay signed in after a refresh. It's strictly necessary for your account to work, and it's removed when you log out.", "Next to it the app keeps which of your devices this browser is (under literaryworm-device: a random id and your account's id, nothing else), so a refresh or a second tab isn't listed as a new device in Listen on another device; it's removed when you log out too. Passkeys you add are kept by your device, browser or password manager, not by LiteraryWorm in your browser's storage, and logging out doesn't remove them.", "Your cookie choice: Termly keeps the choice you make in the banner, a random identifier for this browser and a count of visits in local storage (under TERMLY_API_CACHE), for up to 12 months, so the banner doesn't ask again on every visit. It sets no cookies. Your browser loads the banner from Termly (app.termly.io) on each visit, so Termly receives those requests, including your IP address, under its own privacy policy.", "The app itself: so LiteraryWorm opens quickly and without a connection, your browser keeps a copy of the app's own files (its pages, code, icons and app manifest). This holds nothing about you. If you turn on notifications in Account → Preferences, your browser also keeps a push subscription for LiteraryWorm; turning them off or logging out removes it.", "Books you save for offline: when you choose Read offline, the book's text, and its audio if you ask, are kept on your device until you remove them from the book's page.", "If you turn on Listen offline on a schedule for an audiobook, the app also keeps a small note of that choice (how many chapters, Wi-Fi only, whether to remove finished ones) with the books, and saves or removes chapters as you listen; it goes when you turn it off or the books are cleared.", "Next to them the app keeps a small note of which account's offline place this device holds (a random id, not your name or email), so the place can be freed from another device; it goes when the books do.", "Books you upload: once you've opened one, or searched your uploads by keywords, its text is kept on that device so it keeps working if your connection drops. It's removed when you delete the book or log out.", "Everything else, such as reader settings, playback speed, the demo household and uploads made in the demo, stays in the open tab only and is gone when you refresh or close it.", "Your library, reading progress, bookmarks, highlights, saved words, lists, Up next queue, ratings, reading time and pace, quiz scores, player settings, club activity and uploaded books are saved with your account on our servers (Supabase), not in browser cookies. The Privacy Policy says how we use them, and Account lets you download or delete them.", "Listening on a speaker or TV uses your browser's own Chromecast or AirPlay picker: it loads no script, sets no cookie and keeps nothing in your browser, and the speaker plays the recording straight from the Internet Archive."]}, {"heading": "What else your browser loads from other sites", "paragraphs": ["Some parts of LiteraryWorm load straight from other companies' servers, so your browser sends them requests, with your IP address, and they handle them under their own policies.", "Google Fonts (fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com) serves the app's typefaces. Google Books (books.google.com) serves covers and the preview, and sets the cookies described above.", "The Internet Archive (archive.org) streams LibriVox recordings and audio clips. Termly (app.termly.io) serves the consent banner.", "When you pay, you go to Stripe's own checkout page, where Stripe sets its own cookies under its privacy policy. We never see your card details.", "OpenDyslexic, the reading font, is part of the app itself, so choosing it sends nothing to anyone.", "Ahrefs (analytics.ahrefs.com) serves our visit counter, which loads only if you allow analytics. It sets no cookies and keeps nothing in your browser, and it's off for Kids profiles and on pages whose links carry codes. The Privacy Policy, section 4, says what it receives."]}, {"heading": "Do my choices apply on all my devices?", "paragraphs": ["No. Cookie settings you make in your browser, and anything you clear there, apply to that browser on that device only. You'll need to make them again on a different device or browser, and a private or incognito window deletes what it kept when you close it.", "Clearing LiteraryWorm's site data signs you out, and books saved for offline will need saving again."]}, {"heading": "How can I control cookies on my browser?", "paragraphs": ["As the means by which you can refuse cookies through your web browser controls vary from browser to browser, you should visit your browser's help menu for more information.", "That's true of the most popular browsers: Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge and Opera. In each, you can block third-party cookies for every site, or clear the cookies and site data of one site.", "We don't use advertising networks, so there's nothing to opt out of here. If you'd like to opt out of targeted advertising elsewhere, most advertising networks offer a way to: see the Digital Advertising Alliance, the Digital Advertising Alliance of Canada or the European Interactive Digital Advertising Alliance."]}, {"heading": "Local network access", "paragraphs": ["Your browser may ask whether LiteraryWorm can reach devices on your local network, such as others on your home Wi-Fi.", "Choose Block: the app never needs it, and allowing it can stop logging in from working in that browser. If you allowed it, turn it off in the site's settings (in Chrome and Edge, select the icon beside the address, then Site settings, and set Local network access to Block)."]}, {"heading": "What about other tracking technologies, like web beacons?", "paragraphs": ["Cookies are not the only way to recognize or track visitors to a website. Web beacons (sometimes called \"tracking pixels\" or \"clear gifs\") are tiny graphics files that contain a unique identifier that lets a website recognize when someone has visited it or opened an email including them.", "LiteraryWorm doesn't use web beacons or tracking pixels on the Website or in its emails. We send our emails with Mailgun's open and click tracking turned off."]}, {"heading": "Do you serve targeted advertising?", "paragraphs": ["No. We don't serve advertising on our Website, and we don't allow third parties to place cookies or web beacons on your computer or mobile device through our Website to serve or measure advertising. Google's cookies from books.google.com are set under Google's own policies; we don't use them, and we don't receive them."]}, {"heading": "How often will you update this Cookie Policy?", "paragraphs": ["We may update this Cookie Policy from time to time in order to reflect, for example, changes to the cookies we use or for other operational, legal, or regulatory reasons. Please therefore revisit this Cookie Policy regularly to stay informed about our use of cookies and related technologies.", "The Last updated date at the top of this Cookie Policy shows when it last changed. When a change matters, we'll also say so on What's new."]}, {"heading": "Where can I get further information?", "paragraphs": ["If you have any questions about our use of cookies or other technologies, please email us at support@literaryworm.com. For privacy requests, email usprivacy@literaryworm.com."]}]$lw$::jsonb, updated_at = now()
where slug = $lw$cookies$lw$ and sections = $lw$[{"heading": "In short", "paragraphs": ["We use no advertising or analytics cookies, and we don't track you across other websites.", "Two kinds of cookie are set when you use LiteraryWorm, both by other companies: Cloudflare's bot check on our backend, and Google's cookies from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load.", "Your browser also keeps your sign-in, which device it is, your cookie choice, the app's own files and any books you save for offline. The list below says what each one is and how long it stays."]}, {"heading": "About this policy", "paragraphs": ["This Cookie Policy covers cookies and the similar things your browser keeps for LiteraryWorm, such as local storage and the app's offline caches. The Privacy Policy explains how we use the information itself.", "Some of what your browser keeps, such as your sign-in session, is linked to your account, so we treat it as personal information."]}, {"heading": "What are cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Cookies are small data files that are placed on your computer or mobile device when you visit a website. Cookies are widely used by website owners in order to make their websites work, or to work more efficiently, as well as to provide reporting information.", "Cookies set by the website owner (in this case, LiteraryWorm) are called \"first-party cookies.\" Cookies set by parties other than the website owner are called \"third-party cookies.\" Third-party cookies enable third-party features or functionality to be provided on or through the website (e.g., advertising, interactive content, and analytics).", "The parties that set these third-party cookies can recognize your computer both when it visits the website in question and also when it visits certain other websites."]}, {"heading": "Everything at a glance", "paragraphs": ["Here is everything that's set or kept in your browser when you use LiteraryWorm, who sets it and how long it stays. Each is explained further down.", "__cf_bm (a cookie, set by Cloudflare on .supabase.co, our backend): keeps out bots. Stays 30 minutes.", "Google's cookies (set by Google on books.google.com): set when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API. Google decides how long they stay; we can't read them.", "literaryworm-session (local storage): keeps you signed in. Stays until you log out.", "literaryworm-device (local storage): which of your devices this browser is, as a random id and your account id. Stays until you log out.", "TERMLY_API_CACHE (local storage, kept by Termly): your cookie choice. Stays up to 12 months.", "The app's files (the lw-shell cache): so the app opens quickly and offline. Replaced when the app updates.", "Books saved for offline (the lw-books cache): stay until you remove them, with a small note of your offline place and any listening schedule.", "Books you uploaded and opened, or searched by keywords (the lw-uploads cache): stay until you delete the book or log out.", "A push subscription, only if you turn on notifications on this device: stays until you turn them off or log out."]}, {"heading": "Why do we use cookies?", "paragraphs": ["Some cookies are required for technical reasons in order for our Website to operate, and we refer to these as \"essential\" or \"strictly necessary\" cookies. LiteraryWorm uses only essential cookies and similar technologies, including the third-party cookies Google sets from books.google.com when Google Books covers and previews load, because we use the Google Books API.", "We don't use cookies to track or target your interests, and we don't let third parties serve cookies through our Website for advertising or analytics. This is described in more detail below."]}, {"heading": "How can I control cookies?", "paragraphs": ["You have the right to decide whether to accept or reject cookies. Essential cookies cannot be rejected as they are strictly necessary to provide you with services.", "Whe